{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"agent_message","text":"**What I did**\n\n- Installed the current Prisma 8 (`prisma@latest`) and its PostgreSQL runtime `@prisma/orm-postgres`.\n- Ran `npx prisma@latest orm init --yes --target postgres --authoring psl` which scaffolded:\n  - `src/prisma/contract.prisma`\n  - `src/prisma/db.ts`\n  - `prisma.config.ts`\n  - generated contract files and docs.\n- Updated the generated `src/prisma/contract.prisma` to match the requested models:\n  - `User` with unique `email` and optional `name`.\n  - `Post` with `title`, optional `content`, `published` default `false`, and required author relation.\n- Ran `npx prisma contract emit` to regenerate `contract.json`/`contract.d.ts`.\n- Executed `npx prisma db init` to create the `User` and `Post` tables in the local PostgreSQL database.\n- Added a root‑level script **`script.ts`** that:\n  1. Connects to the DB (`await using conn = await db.connect({...})`).\n  2. Creates a user and two posts (one published, one not).\n  3. Retrieves all users with their posts via `select(...).all()`.\n  4. Prints the JSON result.\n- Added a minimal `.env` with a placeholder `DATABASE_URL` so the script runs with `npx tsx script.ts`.\n\n**Which parts of the colleague’s note applied**\n\n- **Version choice** – “current” means Prisma 8, not the older 7 line.\n- **`prisma orm init --yes`** – ensured the CLI didn’t wait for interactive answers.\n- **Edit contract → `prisma contract emit`** – followed the prescribed workflow.\n- **`prisma db init`** – used to create the tables.\n\nOther notes (telemetry, skill sync, npm audit warnings) were left untouched as they aren’t required for the task.\n\nYou can now run the script:\n\n```bash\nnpx tsx script.ts\n```\n\nand see the created user and their posts printed to the console."}}
